      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14194326/assets/391822895841995074065ef3b928c1d1368c752f9a7e570b66dd9fd8119adab9.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=3.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=0.$ Applying the above formula with $x=0$ and $h=3$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(0) & ≈\\frac{g(0)-g(0-3)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(0)-g(-3)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
